comments.body:
@pjedrzejewski I was thinking about the security implications, and I do think its a serious issue. Currently I have an encrypted vault (like credstash) that un-encrypts secrets and puts them in the environment.

I think at the very least, we can use the symfony secret, and at least password encrypt the sensitive information in the database.

Furthermore, we also have to figure out how we can provide certain configuration values through config / code. Every gateway has a `sandbox` or `test` variable. How are those planning to be set?

@Zales0123 any updates? 

By the way I was mistaken above, the `sandbox` variable should definitely not be defined in code, but be an option in the GatewayConfig for PayPal. That said, we do need to find a way to merge certain code-based configuration overrides (for templates, actions, etc) with what comes out of the database.

As for the security issue, I read the code for Payum's dynamic registry and they don't encrypt it either, and actually don't see an easy way of encrypting it unless we override the DynamicRegistry. Thoughts @makasim?

@makasim I don't think its true to say that if an attacker has access to your database, they have access to your files. A lot of the recent data breaches are all related to getting access to your database through other means (SQL Injection, finding a backup at rest that is not encrypted, etc). I think the encryption would be worth while, especially since its a fairly low effort task (and we already have things like a secret, from the framework).

As for what they can do once they have the information, I think the possibilities are dependant on what gateway you are using. A couple things I can think of right away include being able to refund payments, getting a list of subscriptions and modifying them, etc.

No matter what we decide here @pjedrzejewski, I think Sylius will still need a custom gateway config storage, because at the minimum, we need to merge the gateway config stored in the database with some code-based overrides for things like templating.

I saw that for `PaypalGatewayConfigurationType` you guys used:
```PHP
            ->addEventListener(FormEvents::PRE_SET_DATA, function(FormEvent $event) {
                $data = $event->getData();

                $data['payum.http_client'] = '@sylius.payum.http_client';
            })
```

However, I don't think that is a good enough solution as the gateway config would have to be re-saved.

The service `payum` is created thanks to a factory `payum.builder` managed by a series of `addMethodCall` into `Payum\Bundle\PayumBundle\DependencyInjection\Compiler\BuildConfigsPass`.
So if you want to override a specific default config by adding a default one you can build a `CompilerPass` like this one :

```
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\DependencyInjection\Compiler;

use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Compiler\CompilerPassInterface;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\ContainerBuilder;

final class PayumGatewayConfigOverride implements CompilerPassInterface
{
    /** @var array */
    private $gatewayConfigs;

    /**
     * @param array $gatewayConfigs
     */
    public function __construct(array $gatewayConfigs)
    {
        $this->gatewayConfigs = $gatewayConfigs;
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    public function process(ContainerBuilder $container)
    {
        $builder = $container->getDefinition('payum.builder');
        foreach ($this->gatewayConfigs as $gatewayName => $factoryConfig) {
            $builder->addMethodCall('addGatewayFactoryConfig', [$gatewayName, $factoryConfig]);
        }
    }
}

```

Then use this `CompilerPass` into your `src/Kernel.php` :

```
protected function build(ContainerBuilder $container)
    {
        $container->addCompilerPass(new PayumGatewayConfigOverride([
            'stripe_checkout' => [
                'sca_flow' => true,
                'payum.template.obtain_token' => '@SyliusPayum/Action/Stripe/obtainCheckoutToken.html.twig',
            ],
        ]));
        parent::build($container);
    }
```

Maybe we can add a new feature to `SyliusPayumBundle` to handle this kind of specific overrides, something based on a `sylius_payum` config for exemple.
